   100|## Frame Sequence Export
   101|
   102|### saveFrames()
   103|
   104|```javascript
   105|function keyPressed() {
   106|  if (key === 'f') {
   107|    saveFrames('frame', 'png', 10, 30);
   108|    // 10 seconds, 30 fps → 300 PNG files
   109|    // Downloads as individual files (browser may block bulk downloads)
   110|  }
   111|}
   112|```
   113|
   114|### Manual Frame Export (More Control)
   115|
   116|```javascript
   117|let recording = false;
   118|let frameNum = 0;
   119|const TOTAL_FRAMES = 300;
   120|
   121|function keyPressed() {
   122|  if (key === 'r') recording = !recording;
   123|}
   124|
   125|function draw() {
   126|  // ... render frame ...
   127|
   128|  if (recording) {
   129|    saveCanvas('frame-' + nf(frameNum, 4), 'png');
   130|    frameNum++;
   131|    if (frameNum >= TOTAL_FRAMES) {
   132|      recording = false;
   133|      noLoop();
   134|      console.log('Recording complete: ' + frameNum + ' frames');
   135|    }
   136|  }
   137|}
   138|```
   139|
   140|### Deterministic Capture (Critical for Video)
   141|
   142|The `noLoop()` + `redraw()` pattern is **required** for frame-perfect headless capture. Without it, p5's draw loop runs freely in Chrome while Puppeteer screenshots are slow — the sketch runs ahead and you get duplicate/missing frames.
   143|
   144|```javascript
   145|function setup() {
   146|  createCanvas(1920, 1080);
   147|  pixelDensity(1);
   148|  noLoop();                    // STOP the automatic draw loop
   149|  window._p5Ready = true;      // Signal to capture script
   150|}
   151|
   152|function draw() {
   153|  // This only runs when redraw() is called by the capture script
   154|  // frameCount increments exactly once per redraw()
   155|}
   156|```
   157|
   158|The bundled `scripts/export-frames.js` detects `window._p5Ready` and switches to deterministic mode automatically. Without it, falls back to timed capture (less precise).
   159|
   160|### ffmpeg: Frames to MP4
   161|
   162|```bash
   163|# Basic encoding
   164|ffmpeg -framerate 30 -i frame-%04d.png -c:v libx264 -pix_fmt yuv420p output.mp4
   165|
   166|# High quality
   167|ffmpeg -framerate 30 -i frame-%04d.png \
   168|  -c:v libx264 -preset slow -crf 18 -pix_fmt yuv420p \
   169|  output.mp4
   170|
   171|# With audio
   172|ffmpeg -framerate 30 -i frame-%04d.png -i audio.mp3 \
   173|  -c:v libx264 -c:a aac -shortest \
   174|  output.mp4
   175|
   176|# Loop for social media (3 loops)
   177|ffmpeg -stream_loop 2 -i output.mp4 -c copy output-looped.mp4
   178|```
   179|
   180|### Video Export Gotchas
   181|
   182|**YUV420 clips dark values.** H.264 encodes in YUV420 color space, which rounds dark RGB values. Content below RGB(8,8,8) may become pure black. Subtle dark details (dim particle trails, faint noise textures) disappear in the encoded video even though they're visible in the PNG frames.
   183|
   184|**Fix:** Ensure minimum brightness of ~10 for any visible content. Test by encoding a few frames and comparing the MP4 frame vs the source PNG.
   185|
   186|```bash
   187|# Extract a frame from MP4 for comparison
   188|ffmpeg -i output.mp4 -vf "select=eq(n\,100)" -vframes 1 check.png
   189|```
   190|
   191|**Static frames look broken in video.** If an algorithm produces a single static image (like a pre-computed attractor heatmap), it reads as a freeze/glitch in video. Always add animation even to static content:
   192|- Progressive reveal (expand from center, sweep across)
   193|- Slow parameter drift (rotate color mapping, shift noise offset)
   194|- Camera-like motion (slow zoom, slight pan)
   195|- Overlay animated particles or grain
   196|
   197|**Scene transitions are mandatory.** Hard cuts between visually different scenes are jarring. Use fade envelopes:
   198|
   199|```javascript
   200|const FADE_FRAMES = 15;  // half-second at 30fps
   201|let fade = 1;
   202|if (localFrame < FADE_FRAMES) fade = localFrame / FADE_FRAMES;
   203|if (localFrame > SCENE_FRAMES - FADE_FRAMES) fade = (SCENE_FRAMES - localFrame) / FADE_FRAMES;
   204|fade = fade * fade * (3 - 2 * fade);  // smoothstep
   205|// Apply: multiply all alpha/brightness by fade
   206|```
   207|
   208|### Per-Clip Architecture (Multi-Scene Videos)
   209|
   210|For videos with multiple scenes, render each as a separate HTML file + MP4 clip, then stitch with ffmpeg. This enables re-rendering individual scenes without touching the rest.
   211|
   212|**Directory structure:**
   213|```
   214|project/
   215|├── capture-scene.js          # Shared: node capture-scene.js <html> <outdir> <frames>
   216|├── render-all.sh             # Renders all + stitches
   217|├── scenes/
   218|│   ├── 00-intro.html         # Each scene is self-contained
   219|│   ├── 01-particles.html
   220|│   ├── 02-noise.html
   221|│   └── 03-outro.html
   222|└── clips/
   223|    ├── 00-intro.mp4          # Each clip rendered independently
   224|    ├── 01-particles.mp4
   225|    ├── 02-noise.mp4
   226|    ├── 03-outro.mp4
   227|    └── concat.txt
   228|```
   229|
   230|**Stitch clips with ffmpeg concat:**
   231|```bash
   232|# concat.txt (order determines final sequence)
   233|file '00-intro.mp4'
   234|file '01-particles.mp4'
   235|file '02-noise.mp4'
   236|file '03-outro.mp4'
   237|
   238|# Lossless stitch (all clips must have same codec/resolution/fps)
   239|ffmpeg -f concat -safe 0 -i concat.txt -c copy final.mp4
   240|```
   241|
   242|**Re-render a single scene:**
   243|```bash
   244|node capture-scene.js scenes/01-particles.html clips/01-particles 150
   245|ffmpeg -y -framerate 30 -i clips/01-particles/frame-%04d.png \
   246|  -c:v libx264 -preset slow -crf 16 -pix_fmt yuv420p clips/01-particles.mp4
   247|# Then re-stitch
   248|ffmpeg -y -f concat -safe 0 -i clips/concat.txt -c copy final.mp4
   249|```
   250|
   251|**Re-order without re-rendering:** Just change the order in concat.txt and re-stitch. No frames need re-rendering.
   252|
   253|**Each scene HTML must:**
   254|- Call `noLoop()` in setup and set `window._p5Ready = true`
   255|- Use `frameCount`-based timing (not `millis()`) for deterministic output
   256|- Handle its own fade-in/fade-out envelope
   257|- Be fully self-contained (no shared state between scenes)
   258|
   259|### ffmpeg: Frames to GIF (Better Quality)
   260|
   261|```bash
   262|# Generate palette first for optimal colors
   263|ffmpeg -i frame-%04d.png -vf "fps=15,palettegen=max_colors=256" palette.png
   264|
   265|# Render GIF using palette
   266|ffmpeg -i frame-%04d.png -i palette.png \
   267|  -lavfi "fps=15 [x]; [x][1:v] paletteuse=dither=bayer:bayer_scale=3" \
   268|  output.gif
   269|```
